{"cross_mech":[],"family":{"alphafold_models":4684,"candidate_reasons":["pfam_previous_unknown_name"],"characterization_status":"","context_evidence_count":null,"cross_mech":{"example_proteins":0,"protein_traits_record":false,"records_by_mech":{},"scanned":false},"curation_status":"SEEDED","demotion_reasons":[],"description":"It is vital for effective cell-replication that replication is not stalled at any point by, for instance, damaged bases. Replication termination factor 2 (Rtf2) stabilizes the replication fork stalled at the site-specific replication barrier RTS1 by preventing replication restart until completion of DNA synthesis by a converging replication fork initiated at a flanking origin. The RTS1 element terminates replication forks that are moving in the cen2-distal direction while allowing forks moving in the cen2-proximal direction to pass through the region. Rtf2 contains a C2HC2 motif related to the C3HC4 RING-finger motif, and would appear to fold up, creating a RING finger-like structure but forming only one functional Zn2+ ion-binding site [[cite:PUB00066779]]. This domain is also found at the N-terminus of peptidyl-prolyl cis-trans isomerase 4, a divergent cyclophilin family [[cite:PUB00062056]].","domain_architectures":92,"interpro_id":"","known_evidence_count":null,"matches":5538,"name":"Rtf2 RING-finger","partial_evidence_count":null,"pfam_id":"PF04641","proteins":5475,"proteomes":3338,"short_name":"Rtf2","source_url":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/interpro/api/entry/pfam/PF04641","structures":0,"taxa":8851,"unknown_status":"EX_DUF"},"inputs":{"cross_mech":"cross-mech-duf-examples-2026-10-10","members":"pfam-uniprot-uniref90-2026-10-07","protein_examples":"duf-protein-examples-direct-2026-10-13","worklist":"interpro-pfam-duf-2026-10-10"},"members":[],"metadata":{"curation_status":"SEEDED","local_source":"source/data/families/PF04641.yaml","record":{"characterization_status":"UNSCORED","counters":{"alphafold_models":4684,"domain_architectures":92,"matc